Attached 3d objects inside bones transformation

The new exporter importer now includes:

std::vector<AnimationAttachment> attachments;

So each animation clip has

struct AttachmentAnimationKeyPose {
  int blenderFrame = 0;
  glm::vec3 location{};
  glm::quat rotation{1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f};
  glm::vec3 scale{1.0f};
};

struct AnimationAttachment {
  std::string objectName;
  std::string boneName;
  std::vector<AttachmentAnimationKeyPose> keyPoses;
};


So the logic in a high level is:

- Each object that is an attachment should have maybe like a component that tell us who are attachment of (using entity id I guess is best)
- Because this object has this component, we should be able to get:
    - entity id of the parent.
    - all the animation clips of the parent.
    - all the attachments of all the animation clips.
    - So the attached object should have a list of index of animations where it is attached. Also a list of attachment index per animation clip (initial will be just 0).
    - At that moment the attached component should have everything needed for applying transformation based on the animation.

- All of the above can be set before the game loop once.

So the  component would look like:

{
entity
parentEntity
animationIndices[] //which animations of the parent is this part of
attachmentIndices[] //which of the parent animation and attachments is this part of
}

So the animation system can handle this. Init will be just filling all of that information of every entity with AttachmentAnimationComponent

Update will be:

Per each entity with AttachmentAnimationComponent

- Get the animation component of the parent entity (that will give us all the animation clips)
- Loop trhough all the animationIndices. Per each one, loop trhough all the attachmentIndices. Per each one:

    - You end up getting:
    struct AttachmentAnimationKeyPose {
        int blenderFrame = 0;
        glm::vec3 location{};
        glm::quat rotation{1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f};
        glm::vec3 scale{1.0f};
    };
    - Use that to apply transformation to the attached object.
